# Queue sweep

**This is report-only: a run changes no labels, issues, comments or files in the repo.
It reports gaps to the owner and the coordinator, who decide what to queue (SDLC-023), including check 5's own gap: a ticket that's neither handed out nor queued.**

Run the sweep in a mechanical-tier sub-agent, whatever tier the calling thread runs on; the parent thread reads only the returned report.
This skill is the authorisation for spawning that sub-agent (SDLC-010).

Every stage of this repo's pipeline (`.forge/feature-pipeline.md`, `post-merge-review` and the triage labels) writes a record the next one points at (a triage comment, a run log row, a checklist tick, a label), and each is a small, separate action that can be skipped without anything downstream failing loudly.
A stage 8 Strong finding is called out but never ticketed; a post-merge review Must survives in an archive comment with no sub-issue; a checklist item is ticked by hand ahead of the state it describes; an issue sits with no label at all.
This sweep catches that class of gap; it never fixes anything itself.

## Cadence

Run it as the first step of cutting a release, before the release notes are drafted for real (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`, "Releases").
The Main Health thread also runs it on a standing schedule: see `.agents/skills/watch-main-ci/SKILL.md`'s Routine 1 for the cadence (the first daily firing of the existing trigger) and its "Per-repo values" table for where reports land.

## Report format

Write the report to `queue-sweep/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<shortsha>.md` in the working folder ({{ working_folder }}), using the date and `origin/main`'s short SHA at run time.
Post it in the running thread, and send it to the coordinator where the harness provides a way to message the coordinator.
**When run pre-release**, also post the report as a comment on the release ticket (SDLC-101): that comment, not the working-folder copy, is the durable record of the sweep outcome and the owner's per-gap decisions.
**A standing run** (the Main Health thread's own schedule) stays in the working folder only: it is not a scheduled assurance pass for SDLC-103, so it gets no hosting-platform record.

1. A header line: the UTC time and the owner's local time ({{ owner_timezone }}), the `origin/main` SHA this run read, and "nothing was changed."
2. One section per check below, **in order**, as a table: `#`, title, labels, gap, evidence (the query row or a comment link).
   A check with no gaps still gets its section, reading "none"; never omit a check because it found nothing.
3. **Not checkable this run**: each check (or part of a check) that couldn't run, and why, for example check 7 when the coordinator skill isn't on `origin/main`, or check 5 without the harness's thread-listing tools.
   Never report one of these as a drift finding.
4. **Suggested actions for the coordinator.**

## Query conventions

Queries are `gh api` REST calls, written in full with `per_page=100` and `--paginate` so no result is silently truncated to the platform's default page size.
Where only a hosting-platform MCP tool works (some sandboxes run `gh` with no working token), use its equivalent listing or read call; the query shape stays the same.
A REST issue listing includes pull requests; every query below either filters `.pull_request` directly or says to drop rows carrying it.
`--paginate` applies `--jq` to each page separately, so a filter that reduces the whole result (`last`, `length`, `first`) gives one answer per page: stream elements with `.[] | ...` and reduce outside jq instead.
Where a proxy or token refuses the next-page links `--paginate` follows, page explicitly with `&page=N` on the `repos/...` path until a page comes back with fewer than 100 entries.

**A semantic search tool is not a literal one.**
An MCP issue-search tool may rank by meaning, returning false positives for an exact `in:title` phrase or nothing for an `in:body` phrase that is provably present.
Cross-check any exact-phrase result from such a tool: on a zero result or any doubt, paginate the plain issue listing and filter titles and bodies for the literal substring in a script.

The shell variables below stand for this repo's values:

```
R=repos/{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}
PMR={{ post_merge_review_tracker or "<post_merge_review_tracker in .copier-answers.yml>" }}
IDEAS={{ deferred_ideas_epic or "<deferred_ideas_epic in .copier-answers.yml>" }}
HANGING={{ hanging_epic or "<hanging_epic in .copier-answers.yml>" }}
ARCH={{ arch_findings_epic or "<arch_findings_epic in .copier-answers.yml>" }}
LFS={{ local_full_suite_evidence_tracker or "<local_full_suite_evidence_tracker in .copier-answers.yml>" }}
```

"Ready label" means `ready-for-build`, `ready-for-structure` or `ready-for-human`. `ready-for-feature-design` is a state role, but never a queueable ready label: see check 5.

The operational safety rules are `post-merge-review`'s (`.agents/skills/post-merge-review/SKILL.md`, "Operational safety rules").
This skill makes only read-only API calls, so most don't apply, but the no-paid-calls-without-an-explicit-ask rule still does if a check is dispatched to a sub-agent.

## The fourteen checks

### 1. Stage 8 Strong findings with no ticket

```
gh api -X GET search/issues --paginate -f per_page=100 \
  -f q='repo:{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }} is:issue in:title "Stage 8 architecture pass for"' \
  --jq '.items[] | {number, state, title}'
```

For each pass ticket that is **closed**:

- Find its feature issue: `gh api $R/issues/<n>/parent`.
- Read the feature's comments: `gh api --paginate "$R/issues/<feature>/comments?per_page=100"`.
- Take the comment whose body matches `^## Stage 8 architecture pass`.
- Within that comment, take the lines from the first line matching `^\*\*Strong` up to the next line matching `^\*\*`.
  Each `- ` bullet there is one Strong finding.

Report as gaps:

- (a) a closed pass ticket whose feature has no matching triage comment;
- (b) a Strong bullet with no issue reference;
- (c) a referenced issue that is open with no ready label (`gh api $R/issues/<N> --jq '[.labels[].name]'`);
- (d) any other issue the triage comment references that carries `needs-decision` (a Strong bullet's own issue is (e), never this clause), is open, is not labelled `hanging`, `deferred-accepted-risk`, `future` or `deferred-arch-finding`, and is not among the feature's sub-issues (`gh api --paginate "$R/issues/<feature>/sub_issues?per_page=100"`): the pass must file these as tracker sub-issues (`.forge/feature-pipeline.md`, stage 8).
  A closed issue is fine; a parked one is fine only once the owner's decision comment is on it (`.forge/feature-pipeline.md`, "A ticket is resolved when"): check with `gh api --paginate "$R/issues/<n>/comments?per_page=100" --jq '.[] | select(.author_association == "OWNER")'`, and treat no match as a gap, including a ticket a thread parked and detached on its own.
- (e) a Strong bullet's referenced issue that is open and labelled `hanging`, `deferred-accepted-risk`, `future` or `deferred-arch-finding`: a Strong finding is fixed or escalated, never parked (owner decision, 2026-11-02), so this is a gap whatever decision comment is on it; (d)'s parking exemption and decision-comment check apply only to a `needs-decision` ticket, never to a Strong finding's own issue.

A referenced issue that is closed is fine: it was actioned.

### 2. Post-merge review Musts and Should bundles with no ticket

Read the tracker's body: `gh api $R/issues/$PMR --jq .body`.
Parse the table under its `## Run log` heading by splitting each row on `|`.
Take the Must, Should, Sub-issues and Archive-link column positions from that table's own header row each run rather than hardcoding an index (the generated tracker starts with `Run | Window (merged) | PRs checked | Must | Should | Sub-issues | Archive`).

Limit the rows to runs dated on or after the last release tag's date (`git log -1 --format=%cs $(git tag --sort=-v:refname | head -1)`).
When this check runs as the first step of a release, use every row since the tag before that one instead, so a run from before the prior tag isn't re-flagged as new.
With no tags yet, use every row.

For each row with Must + Should > 0:

- Let `refs` be the distinct issue numbers mentioned in the Sub-issues column.
- The row is a gap if `refs` is empty, if `|refs| < Must` (not enough tickets for the Musts, which each need their own), or if `Should > 0` while `|refs| <= Must` (no separate Should bundle was ever filed; `post-merge-review` files Musts and Should bundles as separate tickets).
- For each issue in `refs`, it's a further gap if it is open with none of a ready label, `deferred-arch-finding`, or `needs-decision` (an out-of-scope or worth-exploring finding raised to the owner).
- For a flagged row, open its archive comment (`gh api $R/issues/comments/<id>`, from the row's Archive link) and quote the Must and Should headings it names as evidence, so the owner sees what went unfiled without re-deriving it.

Also check feature-scoped passes: `gh api -X GET search/issues --paginate -f per_page=100 -f q='repo:{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }} is:issue is:closed in:title "Post-merge review pass for"'`.
Apply the same refs test to each one's closing comment.

### 3. Feature-pipeline checklist drift and finished epics

```
gh api -X GET search/issues --paginate -f per_page=100 \
  -f q='repo:{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }} is:issue is:open in:body "## Feature pipeline"'
```

For each result:

- Parse lines matching `^- \[( |x)\] (\d)\. ` into item number and ticked state; keep any trailing annotation such as `(skipped — single-slice design)`.
- Get its sub-issues: `gh api --paginate "$R/issues/<n>/sub_issues?per_page=100"`.
- Split the sub-issues into pass tickets (titles starting `Post-merge review pass for` or `Stage 8 architecture pass for`) and build slices (everything else).

Report as a gap:

- item 6 ticked but no `Post-merge review pass for <feature>` ticket exists (check the sub-issues and a repo-wide exact-title search, since `feature-pipeline` job (b) creates and links the ticket in two steps that can land out of step);
- item 6 ticked, no `Stage 8 architecture pass for <feature>` ticket exists, and item 9 carries no single-slice-skip annotation;
- item 7 unticked while every build slice is closed with `state_reason: completed`;
- item 8 unticked while the post-merge review pass ticket is closed;
- item 9 unticked while the stage 8 ticket and every Strong-finding and `needs-decision` ticket it filed are resolved (closed, or a `needs-decision` ticket the owner parked, with an owner decision comment on it (check (d)'s command) and detached from the tracker so it no longer appears among the sub-issues);
- every item ticked and every sub-issue closed, but the feature issue itself is still open.

Separately, list open epics sitting at 100%:

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=epic&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | select(.sub_issues_summary.percent_completed==100) | {number,title}'
```

Exclude the standing trackers `.forge/tickets-and-branches.md` names (`$PMR`, `$IDEAS`, `$HANGING`, `$ARCH`, `$LFS`) and any issue whose title starts `Post-merge review archive`; re-read that file each run rather than trusting this list, since that file owns it.

### 4. Open issues with no state label

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | {number, title, labels: [.labels[].name]}'
```

State labels: `needs-triage`, `needs-info`, `needs-grilling`, `ready-for-build`, `ready-for-structure`, `ready-for-feature-design`, `ready-for-human`, `future`, `hanging`, `deferred-accepted-risk`, and `deferred-arch-finding` (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`).
Exempt from this check: issues labelled `epic` (check 3 already covers those).
`release:*`, `bug`, `enhancement` and `breaking` are not state labels and don't satisfy this check on their own.

Report these lists:

- open issues with no state label at all;
- open issues carrying any label `docs/agents/triage-labels.md` lists as retired (expected to be empty; a hit means a migration missed an issue or something re-applied an old label).

### 5. Ready tickets not handed out and not in the queue

The ready set is open issues labelled `ready-for-build` or `ready-for-structure`:

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=ready-for-build&state=open&per_page=100"
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=ready-for-structure&state=open&per_page=100"
```

`ready-for-feature-design` is never part of this ready set: a feature tracker carrying it routes to a one-off stage 3 design thread, not the queue, so it is never flagged as "not queued" (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "`ready-for-feature-design`"). Instead, list every open `ready-for-feature-design` tracker with no stage 3 design comment yet, with its age, under its own "awaiting stage 3" heading:

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=ready-for-feature-design&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | {number, title, created_at}'
```

A tracker that already has a stage 3 design comment (one matching stage 3's shape in `.forge/feature-pipeline.md`: facts verified against `origin/main`, the architecture, the decisions, the security section) is a labelling gap, reported separately from the "awaiting stage 3" list: stage 3 has started but didn't remove the label. A tracker genuinely awaiting stage 3 is not a gap; like check 9's `needs-decision`/`needs-grilling` rows, this listing keeps it from sitting unnoticed between stages 2 and 3.

Drop:

- blocked tickets: `gh api $R/issues/<n> --jq .issue_dependencies_summary.blocked_by` greater than 0, or a `Blocked by:` line in the body naming an issue that's still open;
- the pass tickets check 3 already found still blocked.

A remaining ticket counts as handed out if any of these holds:

- (a) it has an assignee;
- (b) an open PR references it: `gh api -X GET search/issues -f q='repo:{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }} is:pr is:open <n> in:body'`, confirmed by a `Ticket: #<n>` or `Closes #<n>` line in the PR body (not just the number appearing incidentally);
- (c) it carries the `in-flight` label;
- (d) a build-slot thread (named `Build <slot> <repo>`, per the coordinator core's "Naming") is working, blocked or ready for review, and its latest status line or message names the ticket; list threads and read each one's current status with the harness's thread tools, since a slot thread's title doesn't carry the issue number.

"In the coordinator's queue" means the ticket carries the `queued` label, or its number appears in the newest coordinator message in the project chat that lists queued tickets (read with the harness's chat-reading tool, newest first, until that message is found).

Report the tickets that are neither handed out nor queued (SDLC-023) as gaps for the owner and the coordinator, like every other check here.
List each under "Suggested actions for the coordinator" as "queue now", since the coordinator's default-queue rule ("The label state machine") already says a ready ticket like this one should be queued without being asked; when the ticket is `needs-decision`, `hanging`, `deferred-accepted-risk`, `future` or `deferred-arch-finding`, carries an open `Blocked by:`, or the owner is on record holding it, say so instead.

Also list open review-pass tickets with no `main-health` label: a title starting `Post-merge review pass`, a title naming full-suite or CI-drift work, or a `Route: Main Health` line in the body.
Expected to be empty; a hit is a labelling gap, fixed by adding `main-health` (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "`main-health`").

**If this harness has no thread-listing or chat-reading tools** (part (d) and the chat read both need them), report "Not checkable this run: queue not fully readable from this harness" instead of treating the residual list as gaps; don't count a ticket as un-queued just because this run couldn't see the queue.

### 6. Hanging-epic children past their revisit date

The population is the union of:

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=hanging&state=open&per_page=100"
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=deferred-accepted-risk&state=open&per_page=100"
gh api --paginate "$R/issues/$HANGING/sub_issues?per_page=100" --jq '.[] | select(.state=="open")'
```

Report a membership mismatch either way (a child of the hanging epic with neither `hanging` nor `deferred-accepted-risk`, or either label on an issue that isn't a child of the epic) as its own list.

`docs/agents/triage-labels.md` sets `hanging`'s and `deferred-accepted-risk`'s revisit trigger as the hanging epic's revisit cadence plus any revisit date or condition recorded in the issue itself.
This skill reads that per-issue date from a one-line `Revisit: YYYY-MM-DD` (or `Revisit by:`/`Revisit on:`), with the pattern `(?im)^\**Revisit(?: by| on)?:?\**\s*(\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2})`, taking the last match in the body, else in the comments, newest first.

Two result lists:

- **past revisit date**: a `Revisit:` date found, and it is before today (UTC);
- **no revisit date recorded**: no `Revisit:` line found anywhere; show the issue's free-text trigger if one is obviously present in the body, otherwise just the issue number and title.
  Don't substitute the epic's blanket cadence for a per-issue date: every child would read overdue at once, hiding which are genuinely due.

If `docs/agents/triage-labels.md` tightens the revisit trigger to a specific format, this `Revisit:` convention follows it.

### 7. Orchestration rules: coordinator skill vs. project settings

The coordinator reads its rules from `.forge/coordinator-core.md`, whose sections cite the source sections they condense. This check has three parts.

**(a) The core is whole and current with its sources.**

```
python3 scripts/check_coordinator_core.py shape
```

A non-zero exit is a gap: the core is over its size limit, missing its truncation guard, or missing its end marker. Quote the script's own report.
In TheForge's own repository only, also run TheForge's `lock` check, which compares each section's cited source sections with the root-only lock file; a consuming project has no lock and reports that part under "Not checkable this run". A failing `lock` check is a gap: a cited source section changed, or a citation broke, without the core being re-read and the lock refreshed.

**(b) The live project settings carry the current coordinator-core section.** Run this part only when this repository is the lead (the owner's `Lead repo:` line) or the only member; a non-lead member reports it under "Not checkable this run: covered by the lead repository's sweep." Write the harness's project instructions, as delivered in this thread's own context, verbatim to `settings.txt`, then:

```
python3 scripts/check_coordinator_core.py drift settings.txt
```

A non-zero exit is a gap, quoted from its report: the live text no longer contains the snippet (`docs/agents/coordinator-core-snippet.md`), or it exceeds the host's size limit. Report a member contradiction with no open `needs-decision` ticket for that member and section as a Should.

**(c) Session-start re-read, best effort.** This check can't verify whether long-running threads were told to re-read the current `CLAUDE.md`, skills and project instructions recently (`.forge/coordinator-core.md`, "Reuse, rotation and resolution"); when a thread's own transcript suggests it hasn't happened in a long time, note it as a Could-only observation, never a finding on its own.

This check does not run, and is reported under "Not checkable this run" rather than as drift, in either of these cases:

- `.forge/coordinator-core.md` isn't on `origin/main` yet, or `scripts/check_coordinator_core.py` doesn't exist yet;
- this run's thread can't see the project instructions at all, in which case the report asks the coordinator to run this one check instead.

### 8. Process changes that cite no SDLC statement

A process change is reviewed against `docs/software-development-lifecycle.md` (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`, "Process changes follow the lifecycle doc"), and the PR's `Process:` line records that review; this check finds the PRs that skipped it.

```
git fetch origin main
git log <last-sweep-sha>..origin/main --format='%H %s' -- CLAUDE.md .forge .agents/skills docs/agents
```

`<last-sweep-sha>` is the short SHA in the most recent report's file name under the report path; with no earlier report, use the commit that generated the repo.
Squash-merge subjects end in `(#<n>)`; fetch each PR's body (`gh api "$R/pulls/<n>" --jq .body`).
A PR is a candidate when its `Process:` line is absent, says `none` or `n/a`, or names statements the diff does not trace to.
Read its diff against the document's numbered statements.
Report it when the diff touches the subject of an `SDLC-` statement, or introduces or changes a policy with an engineering-management effect that no numbered statement justifies and directs, quoting the statement (or noting that none exists) and the hunk.
A process PR that does neither is not a gap.

Skip PRs that changed nothing but the lifecycle document itself; the owner's approval is its record.
Skip PRs whose only process change is a forge sync (`forge-sync`), whose record is the overrides log and the sync PR itself.
If the document isn't on `origin/main`, report the check under "Not checkable this run".

### 9. Open `needs-decision` and `needs-grilling` tickets

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=needs-decision&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | {number, title, created_at}'
```

This is a listing, not a drift check like 1-8: `needs-decision` (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "`needs-decision`") blocks a ticket whatever its other labels, and has no staleness threshold.
Report every result as its own row (`#`, title, labels, age in whole days since `created_at`, UTC, with the query itself as evidence) under its own "awaiting the owner" heading.
Feed that same list into progress reports under "anything awaiting the owner" (`.forge/coordinator-core.md`, "Progress reports", item 5).
An empty result still gets the heading, reading "none".

Run the same query and row shape for `needs-grilling` (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "`needs-grilling`"), excluding a result that also carries `needs-decision`: the two can stack (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "`needs-decision`"), and a ticket carrying both is hard-blocked on the owner, not open for a grilling thread:

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=needs-grilling&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | select([.labels[].name] | index("needs-decision") | not) | {number, title, created_at}'
```

Report it under its own "awaiting a grill" heading, in the same shape; an empty result still gets the heading.
These tickets are work for a grilling thread, not "awaiting the owner", so feed them into progress reports under the queue-left section instead.
A ticket excluded here for also carrying `needs-decision` is not dropped: it appears in the `needs-decision` listing and stays "awaiting the owner" until the owner decides.

### 10. Queue empty only if nothing is queued and no feature epic is open with unowned work

The queue is empty only when both of these lists are empty.
Report each list as its own table with the query as evidence; an empty list reads "none".

**(a) Open tickets carrying a queue label.**

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=queued&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | {number, title, created_at}'
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=in-flight&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | {number, title, created_at}'
```

Report each row with its label.
An `in-flight` ticket whose PR has merged, or whose issue is closed, is a gap: the build thread did not drop its label.

**(b) Open feature epics with unowned open sub-issues.**
A sub-issue counts as owned when it carries `queued` or `in-flight`.
Get the open epics, dropping pull requests and the standing trackers check 3 excludes:

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=epic&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | {number, title}'
```

For each epic that is a feature tracker, list its open sub-issues and keep the ones with neither queue label:

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues/<epic>/sub_issues?per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.state=="open") | select([.labels[].name] | (index("queued") or index("in-flight")) | not) | {number, title}'
```

Each row is a gap: the coordinator must re-read the epic, file or label the sub-issue, and keep the queue open until it is closed.

Also report the outcome in one line: "queue empty" only when (a) and (b) are both none; otherwise, say which list is non-empty.

### 11. Scheduled pass overdue (SDLC-103)

A scheduled pass that leaves no record is read as one that didn't run (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`, the scheduled-passes table).
This check reads the two passes whose evidence is a hosting-platform record a query can check for staleness; the others in that table need no overdue check (a skipped mutation sample or standing-sweep firing correctly leaves nothing, and the scheduled CI dispatch's runs are already visible).

**(a) Post-merge review watermark.**

```
gh api "$R/issues/$PMR" --jq '{body}'
```

Parse the sha and merge timestamp from the body's `**Watermark:**` line, in its one canonical format (`post-merge-review` step 9): `<sha> (PR #<n>, merged <ISO8601>)`.
The PR number is part of that line, but this check doesn't use it.
If the line still reads "none yet", the pass has never run: flag a gap only if any PR has merged at all.

Don't judge staleness by the tracker's `updated_at`: the agent that merges a PR posts a pending note on this tracker as the merge's last step (`post-merge-review`, "Pending reviews"), so `updated_at` moves on every merge whether or not a pass ran.
Read the comments themselves, which are the review's own evidence, paging explicitly rather than with `--paginate`, since some proxies refuse the next-page links it follows ("Query conventions", and part (b) below).

```
page=1
while :; do
  out=$(gh api "$R/issues/$PMR/comments?per_page=100&page=$page") || { echo API-FAILED; break; }
  printf '%s' "$out" | jq -c '.[] | select(.author_association == "OWNER" or .author_association == "MEMBER" or .author_association == "COLLABORATOR") |
    {created_at,
     pending_pr: ((.body | capture("<!-- pending-post-merge-review pr=(?<p>[0-9]+)")).p // null),
     covered: ((.body | capture("<!-- post-merge-review-archive covered=(?<c>[^ ]*) -->")).c // null)}'
  n=$(printf '%s' "$out" | jq 'if type == "array" then length else "x" end')
  case $n in ''|*[!0-9]*) echo API-FAILED; break ;; esac
  [ "$n" -lt 100 ] && break
  page=$((page + 1))
done
```

A marker from any other commenter does not count, pending or archive: on a public repository anyone can post one, and trusting it would let an outside comment silently narrow or widen the review window, or forge a covered-PR claim.
A comment matching neither capture is ignored, including a distinctly-marked note such as `watch-main-ci`'s Routine 2 missing-script note, which is neither a pending note nor an archive comment and must never be mistaken for either.

Comments, and so the lines this loop prints, come back oldest first (part (b) relies on the same ordering).
Each `covered` field is a comma-separated list of PR numbers (`post-merge-review` step 7 puts this marker on every archive comment); union every line's list into one set.
Take the first line with a non-null `covered` as `<first-marked-archive>` and the last as `<latest-marked-archive>` (either is "none" if no archive comment on the tracker carries the marker yet).

A pending note (by its own `pending_pr`) counts as swept if either holds:

- its PR number appears in the unioned `covered` set; or
- `<first-marked-archive>` exists and the note's `created_at` is before it, grandfathering a note from before the marker existed, when every earlier archive comment was unmarked prose this check could only tell from a pending note by elimination.

Once `<first-marked-archive>` exists, a pending note posted after it is swept only by appearing in some `covered` list, never by timestamp alone, so a partial run's deliberately-left-pending notes (`post-merge-review` step 1, "leave substantial newly merged PRs for the next pass") stay unswept rather than being cleared by the next run's archive comment just because it landed later.
Before any marked archive comment exists, a pending note is unswept only if posted after the latest comment of any kind on the tracker.

Flag a gap when either:

- the oldest unswept pending note is more than 3 days old; or
- (independent of whether any pending note exists at all) the oldest PR merged since `<cutoff>` is more than 3 days old:
  ```
  page=1
  : > /tmp/merged-since-cutoff.jsonl
  while :; do
    out=$(gh api "/search/issues?q=repo:{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}+type:pr+is:merged+merged:>=<cutoff>&per_page=100&page=$page") || { echo API-FAILED; break; }
    printf '%s' "$out" | jq -c '.items[].pull_request.merged_at' >> /tmp/merged-since-cutoff.jsonl
    n=$(printf '%s' "$out" | jq '.items | length')
    case $n in ''|*[!0-9]*) echo API-FAILED; break ;; esac
    [ "$n" -lt 100 ] && break
    page=$((page + 1))
  done
  sort /tmp/merged-since-cutoff.jsonl | head -n1
  ```
  `<cutoff>` is the later of `<latest-marked-archive>`'s `created_at` (if one exists) and the watermark line's own `merged <ISO8601>` (unless it reads "none yet"), each taken as the full timestamp, never truncated to a date.
  Use `<latest-marked-archive>`, never `<first-marked-archive>`: anchoring on the first would re-flag the same early PRs as overdue forever, long after a later run's `covered` list handled them.
  Take the earliest `merged_at` across the full result set, not the earliest-created PR's: the search API has no `sort=merged`, and creation order can diverge from merge order enough to mask a real backlog, so page through every match and sort client-side.
  The watermark's own merge time keeps this backstop live on a tracker whose archive comments all predate the marker: otherwise, with no marked archive and no pending notes left, it would report clean while merges pile up with no evidence.
  Skip the backstop only when neither source gives a `<cutoff>` (never run, which the watermark's "none yet" case covers).
  It catches what pending notes alone can't: merges continuing while no new note lands, which would otherwise read as "no unswept note, so not overdue", backwards from SDLC-103's "absence of evidence means the pass didn't run."

No unswept note, no such merge, or neither older than 3 days, is not a gap, however old `updated_at` reads.

**(b) Local full-suite evidence tracker.**
Skip this part, reporting it under "Not checkable this run: no local full-suite script yet", while `{{ local_full_suite_script }}` is not on `origin/main`.

The canonical record is a comment on the local full-suite evidence tracker `$LFS`, never a commit status: some environments' GitHub proxy refuses commit-status writes outright, so reading only statuses would show those environments permanently overdue while the script runs cleanly.
A full run's comment carries a single-line marker `<!-- local-full-suite-evidence sha=<sha> finished=<ISO8601> aggregate=pass|fail -->`; a resumed or partial run posts none, so its absence between two full runs is not a gap.
A marker from any other commenter does not count: on a public repository anyone can post one, and trusting it would let an outside comment plant a false "this ran clean" record.

```
page=1
while :; do
  out=$(gh api "$R/issues/$LFS/comments?per_page=100&page=$page") || { echo API-FAILED; break; }
  printf '%s' "$out" | jq -c '.[] | select((.body | test("<!-- local-full-suite-evidence sha=")) and (.author_association == "OWNER" or .author_association == "MEMBER" or .author_association == "COLLABORATOR"))'
  n=$(printf '%s' "$out" | jq 'if type == "array" then length else "x" end')
  case $n in ''|*[!0-9]*) echo API-FAILED; break ;; esac
  [ "$n" -lt 100 ] && break
  page=$((page + 1))
done | tail -n1
```

Comments come back oldest first, so the last line is the newest marked comment; the loop pages explicitly for both reasons in "Query conventions".
A last line of `API-FAILED` means a call failed or returned something other than a list partway: report this part "not checkable" rather than reading an earlier page's marker as the newest evidence.
Prefer the marker's own `finished=` value to the comment's `created_at`, which records the API call, not necessarily when the run finished.
Parse `sha=`, `finished=` and `aggregate=` out of the marker line (pattern: `sha=(\S+) finished=(\S+) aggregate=(\S+)`); a comment missing any of the three is malformed, equivalent to no evidence for that run.
Compare `finished` to now, and `sha` to `origin/main`'s current head (`git rev-parse origin/main`, or `gh api "$R/commits/main" --jq .sha`).

Flag a gap when either:

- a marked comment was found, it is more than 24 hours old, **and** `main`'s head has moved since that comment's `sha` (a quiet period where `main` hasn't moved is not a gap: there is nothing new to re-verify); or
- no comment on the tracker carries the marker at all (not an API failure, so not "not checkable": an evidence tracker with no full-run evidence is itself the gap).

Where the environment allows commit-status writes, a `{{ lfs_status_context }}` aggregate status usually exists on the same commit too, but this check never reads or depends on it: the tracker comment alone is sufficient everywhere this routine runs.

Report both (a) and (b) as their own rows, each reading "none" when clean.
A gap here is "not checkable this run" only when the API calls themselves fail (rate limit, no token); it is never silently skipped.

### 12. Stale coordinator boot receipt

The boot routine (`.forge/coordinator-core.md`, "Start-of-session self-check") has every coordinator session write a receipt carrying the `origin/main` sha it booted from, as its checklist's first line and appended to `{{ working_folder }}/coordinator/boot-log.md`. This check finds a receipt left behind by a later change to either file the boot routine reads.

In a project with several members, the one coordinator writes this receipt to the lead's working folder: run this check only in the lead's (or the only member's) sweep, gated like 7(b), and read `boot-log.md` from the lead's working folder (its `## Repo facts` row), not this repository's own.

```
tail -n1 {{ working_folder }}/coordinator/boot-log.md
```

Parse `<sha>` out of the line's `@ <sha>;` segment. If the file doesn't exist yet, or its last line doesn't match that shape, report "none yet", not a gap: nothing has booted since this check or the boot routine existed.

```
git fetch origin main
git diff --name-only <sha> origin/main -- .forge/coordinator-core.md .agents/skills/build-thread-brief/SKILL.md
```

A non-empty result is a gap: the coordinator core or the build-thread-brief skill changed on `origin/main` after the receipt's sha, so the receipt no longer reflects what the coordinator read. Flag it as a **Should**, never a Must: the next boot (the next compaction, or the coordinator's next session) picks up the change on its own, so a stale receipt is a staleness gap, not an unsafe one.

**Worked example.** `boot-log.md`'s last line reads `Booted from lead-repo @ a1b2c3d; design/review=opus, build=sonnet, mechanical=haiku; timer armed: yes`. A later commit edits `.forge/coordinator-core.md`'s "Slots and the queue" section, so the `git diff --name-only` above returns `.forge/coordinator-core.md`: flag "boot receipt stale: coordinator core changed since `a1b2c3d`" as a Should. Had that commit touched only an unrelated file (say `docs/principles.md`), the diff would be empty and the receipt still current.

This check does not run, and is reported under "Not checkable this run" rather than as drift, when `.forge/coordinator-core.md`'s "## Start-of-session self-check" section isn't on `origin/main` yet, or when this run's thread can't read the project's shared files at all.

### 13. Post-merge review tracker body corrupted

The tracker's body (`post-merge-review` step 9, "Keeping the tracker tidy") carries only the watermark, the procedure pointer and the run log index; a pending note or archive record is always a comment, never a body edit (`post-merge-review`, "Pending reviews"). A stray body-write that sends a pending note's text as the new body destroys the watermark and run log, and nothing else notices until the next pass is due.

```
gh api "$R/issues/$PMR" --jq '{body}'
```

Flag a gap when either:

- the body does not contain the literal text `**Watermark:**`; or
- the body contains a pending-note or archive marker (`<!-- pending-post-merge-review` or `<!-- post-merge-review-archive`): these belong only in comments, so either one in the body is the signature of this failure, not merely a side effect of a missing watermark.

Either condition is a Must: the tracker is this repo's only durable record of the cadence's progress, and losing it silently is the "absence of evidence" failure SDLC-103 exists to catch, of the record itself. Restore the body from the latest run log entry's archive comment (the last-known-good source), then raise how the body-write happened as its own `needs-decision` finding, so the write path gets fixed, not only its symptom.

A gap here is "not checkable this run" only when the API call itself fails; it is never silently skipped.

### 14. `ready-for-structure` left on after its own design-exit pass is already done

A backstop for the mechanical label sync `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`'s "Mechanical label sync after design-type work" requires at `implement-with-merge` step 0a, catching a ticket where that step was skipped.

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=ready-for-structure&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | {number, title, body}'
```

Flag a result as a gap when its fetched body, or `gh api --paginate "$R/issues/<n>/comments" --jq '.[].body'`, contains a `Structure guidance` heading: the design-exit pass that should have flipped it to `ready-for-build` has already run (`implement-with-merge` step 0a writes the heading into either place).
Report it as a labelling gap, fixed by removing `ready-for-structure` and adding `ready-for-build`, never by re-running the design-exit pass.
A result with no `Structure guidance` heading is still genuinely waiting on its design-exit pass, so it is not a gap.
A feature's own tracker never carries `ready-for-structure`; check 5's "awaiting stage 3" listing is the equivalent backstop for `ready-for-feature-design`.

## Not built, and why

- **No `docs/agents/` reference page.** It would fail the deletion test by repeating this file; every procedure in this family (`post-merge-review`, `watch-main-ci`) keeps its queries in the skill itself.
- **No script or runtime code.** Every check is an agent-run API read; a script would add a coverage obligation and a parser for hand-written prose that an agent reads better.
  If a dry run shows a check's parsing is unreliable by hand, file that as its own ticket rather than building a parser ad hoc.
- **No tracker issue of its own.** This skill keeps no state (no watermark: every run is a full sweep), and never posts to the tracker except a pre-release report on the release ticket.
- **It never edits `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`.** It only reads the labels defined there.
